```

```{r}
#| fig-width: 4
#| fig-height: 3.2
accident_weekend |>
  filter(HOUR >= 0, HOUR <= 23) |>
  count(weekend, HOUR) |>
  group_by(weekend) |>
  mutate(proportion = n / sum(n)) |>
  ggplot(aes(x = HOUR, y = proportion, color = weekend)) +
  geom_line(linewidth = 0.8) +
  geom_point(size = 1.2) +
  scale_y_continuous(labels = percent) +
  scale_x_continuous(breaks = seq(0, 23, 4)) +
  labs(title = "Hourly Distribution", x = "Hour", y = "Proportion", color = "Day Type") +
  theme_minimal(base_size = 10)
```

```{r}
#| fig-width: 4
#| fig-height: 3.2
vehicle_plot |>
  filter(vehicle_type %in% top_vehicle) |>
  ggplot(aes(x = vehicle_type, fill = weekend)) +
  geom_bar(position = "dodge") +
  labs(title = "Vehicle Types by Day Type", x = "Vehicle Type", y = "Vehicles", fill = "Day Type") +
  theme_minimal(base_size = 10) +
  theme(axis.text.x = element_text(angle = 35, hjust = 1, size = 8))
```

:::

::: {.smaller}

After accounting for the different number of days, weekends had more fatal crashes per day.

Weekend crashes were more concentrated in late-night and early-morning hours.

Weekday crashes were relatively more common during daytime and commuting hours.

Sedans and pickups were the most common vehicle types in both groups.
:::

